### What happens?

Using virtual column & a sequence causes `UPDATE` to fail if virtual column(s) is not last.

The following fails:

```sql
DROP SEQUENCE IF EXISTS id_gen CASCADE;
CREATE SEQUENCE id_gen;

CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE fails_to_update (
    virt TEXT GENERATED ALWAYS AS (val::TEXT) VIRTUAL,
    id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT nextval('id_gen'),
    val integer
);

insert into fails_to_update (val) values (42);

update fails_to_update set val = 142 where val = 42 returning *;
```

but if you change virtual column order it works:
```sql
DROP SEQUENCE IF EXISTS id_gen CASCADE;
CREATE SEQUENCE id_gen;

CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE works_to_update (
    id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT nextval('id_gen'),
    val integer,
    virt TEXT GENERATED ALWAYS AS (val::TEXT) VIRTUAL
);

insert into works_to_update (val) values (42);

update works_to_update set val = 142 where val = 42 returning *;
```

Based on some experimentation it is necessary to have a column that use the sequence. I didn't test it but possibly having any default value is enough.


This seems similar to #13471

### To Reproduce

```sql
