See #4308

We should support the `UNION BY NAME` operator to union together tables with re-ordered and potentially new columns. This operator is useful for unifying data from an evolving schema, where new columns have been added over time.

Example usage:

```sql
create table integers(i integer, j integer);
create table strings(j integer, k varchar);
insert into integers values (1, 10);
insert into strings values (20, 'hello world');
SELECT i, j FROM integers UNION BY NAME SELECT j, k FROM strings;
┌──────┬────┬─────────────┐
│  i   │ j  │      k      │
├──────┼────┼─────────────┤
│ 1    │ 10 │ NULL        │
│ NULL │ 20 │ hello world │
└──────┴────┴─────────────┘

-- equivalent to 
SELECT i, j, NULL AS k FROM integers UNION ALL SELECT NULL as i, j, k FROM strings;
```

Internally this should be a simple rewrite in `bind_setop_node.cpp` that pushes a projection to re-order columns, together with inserting constant `NULLs` were required with the appropriate names.

See also [here](https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.1.1/api/python/reference/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.unionByName.html) for how Spark supports this operation.

Creating temporary functions are really useful when generating dynamic SQL.  It would be awesome if this was supported (available in Postgres and Bigquery).

CREATE TEMPORARY MACRO some_func() ... ;

SELECT some_func(..) FROM ..

