- [0.2.8dev744.0--so like a week old?] Have you tried this on the latest `master` branch? In case you cannot compile, you may find some binaries here: https://github.com/duckdb/duckdb/releases/tag/master-builds

Currently we use the following structs for dates/times/timestamps in the C API:

```cpp
typedef struct {
	int32_t year;
	int8_t month;
	int8_t day;
} duckdb_date;

typedef struct {
	int8_t hour;
	int8_t min;
	int8_t sec;
	int32_t micros;
} duckdb_time;

typedef struct {
	duckdb_date date;
	duckdb_time time;
} duckdb_timestamp;
```

This works, but is rather inefficient since the structs do not match our own internal date/time/timestamp representation. It would be better if these would be stored as our own internal representation, and we would expose functions that could extract different components, e.g.:

```cpp
typedef struct {
    int32_t days;
} duckdb_date;

int32_t duckdb_date_years(duckdb_date date);
int32_t duckdb_date_months(duckdb_date date);
int32_t duckdb_date_days(duckdb_date date);
```

#### What happens?
There's no way to append `NULL` values using the `DuckDBAppender` class in Java.

#### To Reproduce

Execute the following code:

```java
import org.duckdb.DuckDBAppender;
import org.duckdb.DuckDBConnection;

import java.sql.*;

class Scratch {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws SQLException {
        try (Connection connection = DriverManager.getConnection("jdbc:duckdb:")) {
            try (Statement statement = connection.createStatement()) {
                statement.executeUpdate("CREATE TABLE messages (id BIGINT, message VARCHAR)");
            }

            try (DuckDBAppender appender = ((DuckDBConnection) connection).createAppender("main", "messages")) {
                appender.beginRow();
                appender.append(0);
                appender.append(null);
                appender.endRow();
            }

            try (Statement statement = connection.createStatement();
                 ResultSet resultSet = statement.executeQuery("SELECT * FROM messages")) {
                while (resultSet.next()) {
                    System.out.printf("id = %d; message = %s%n",
                            resultSet.getInt(1),
                            resultSet.getString(2));
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

This will produce the following exception:

```
Exception in thread "main" java.lang.NullPointerException
	at org.duckdb.DuckDBAppender.append(DuckDBAppender.java:59)
	at Scratch.main(scratch.java:16)
```

This is due to the fact that the `DuckDBAppender.append(String)` method assumes a non-null value and immediately invokes `String.getBytes(Charset)` without a null check: https://github.com/duckdb/duckdb/blob/master/tools/jdbc/src/main/java/org/duckdb/DuckDBAppender.java#L59

Even if this method did support a `null` Java value, the call to the underlying native method would fail because `Java_org_duckdb_DuckDBNative_duckdb_1jdbc_1appender_1append_1string` in `duckdb_java.cpp` similarly assumes a non-null byte array has been passed.

Possible solutions include:

* Allowing `DuckDBAppender.append(String)` to accept a `null` value and insert `NULL` accordingly (regardless of column type)
* Adding an `appendNull()` method to `DuckDBAppender` so clients can explicitly indicate they want a `NULL` value at the current position

