Actual:
src/common/types/column_data_collection.cpp:98: duckdb::Value duckdb::ColumnDataRow::GetValue(duckdb::idx_t) const: Assertion `row_index < chunk.size()' failed.

Expected:
I would expect an unimplemented type for cast error.

### What happens?

`ArrowBuffer::ReserveInternal` in `arrow_buffer.hpp` does not check the return value of `malloc`/`realloc`. When allocation fails (returns `NULL`), `capacity` is still updated to the requested size. Subsequent calls to `reserve()` see sufficient capacity and skip allocation, leaving `dataptr` as `NULL`. Any write to the buffer then dereferences `NULL` → `SIGSEGV`.                                 

`dataptr = realloc(dataptr, bytes)` additionally leaks the original allocation if realloc fails, since the old pointer is overwritten with `NULL`.                                          

Bubbling out, `ResultArrowArrayStreamWrapper::MyStreamGetNext` does not wrap its call to `ArrowUtil::TryFetchChunk` in a `try/catch`, so any exception thrown during Arrow export (e.g., from fixing the above with a `throw`) escapes through the `extern "C"` Arrow callback boundary, causing `terminate()` or an FFI abort.

For reasons I have not identified, the bug is not observed when using GCC 14.1.0.

The segfault only occurs when `SET arrow_output_version = '1.4';` is specified. 

The identification of this bug, and construction of the reproduction, were with the assistance of @claude.  

### To Reproduce

This example was constructed within a DuckDB [extension](https://github.com/the-miint/duckdb-miint) based on the extension template, and assumes DuckDB has been built to link against. The original triggering test was an [edge case](https://github.com/the-miint/duckdb-miint/blob/5757325774a9a03977421a380f25510bfaa7c461/test/sql/rype_extract.test#L242-L252). 

The bug is triggered by constructing a `ResultArrowArrayStreamWrapper` with a large `batch_size` on a query containing a `BLOB` or `VARCHAR` column with `arrow_output_version = '1.4'` (`StringView` format). `ArrowVarcharToStringViewData::Initialize` calls `GetMainBuffer().reserve(capacity * 16)`, and when the resulting allocation request exceeds available memory, `malloc` returns NULL silently.

```c++
// Reproducer: SIGSEGV due to unchecked malloc failure in ArrowBuffer::ReserveInternal
//
// ArrowBuffer::ReserveInternal sets capacity even when malloc returns NULL.
// Subsequent reserve() calls see the large capacity and skip allocation,
// leaving dataptr=NULL. Writing to the buffer then crashes.
//
// The bug is triggered when ResultArrowArrayStreamWrapper is constructed with
// a large batch_size (used as initial_capacity for ArrowAppender). On high-memory
// systems, this can request hundreds of GB, causing malloc to fail silently.
//
// Build (from duckdb-miint repo root, after `bash build.sh`):
//   g++ -std=c++20 -O3 -I duckdb/src/include \
//       repro_arrow_stringview_bug.cpp \
//       -L build/release/src -lduckdb \
//       -Wl,-rpath,build/release/src \
//       -o repro_arrow_stringview_bug
//
// Run:
//   ./repro_arrow_stringview_bug
//
// Expected: error message about allocation failure
// Actual: SIGSEGV (writing to NULL pointer in ArrowVarcharToStringViewData::Append)

#include "duckdb.hpp"
#include "duckdb/common/arrow/result_arrow_wrapper.hpp"

int main() {
    duckdb::DuckDB db(nullptr);
    duckdb::Connection conn(db);

    // Enable Arrow StringView format (v1.4+) — BLOB columns use ArrowVarcharToStringViewData
    auto r1 = conn.Query("SET arrow_output_version = '1.4'");
    if (r1->HasError()) {
        fprintf(stderr, "SET error: %s\n", r1->GetError().c_str());
        return 1;
    }

    // Create a table with a BLOB column
    auto r2 = conn.Query("CREATE TABLE t AS SELECT 'ACGTACGT'::BLOB AS seq");
    if (r2->HasError()) {
        fprintf(stderr, "CREATE error: %s\n", r2->GetError().c_str());
        return 1;
    }

    auto result = conn.Query("SELECT seq FROM t");
    if (result->HasError()) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Query error: %s\n", result->GetError().c_str());
        return 1;
    }

    // Use a very large batch_size (initial_capacity for ArrowAppender).
    // ArrowVarcharToStringViewData::Initialize calls:
    //   GetMainBuffer().reserve(capacity * sizeof(arrow_string_view_t))  // capacity * 16
    //   GetAuxBuffer().reserve(capacity)
    // With capacity=500000000, the aux buffer tries to reserve 500M bytes (fine),
    // but the main buffer tries NextPowerOfTwo(500M * 16 = 8GB) = 8GB.
    // On systems with limited virtual memory, this malloc may fail.
    // ArrowBuffer::ReserveInternal does NOT check for NULL, corrupting the state.
    //
    // To guarantee the failure, use a capacity large enough that malloc returns NULL.
    // 200'000'000'000 * 16 = 3.2 TB for main buffer — will fail on any system.
    size_t huge_batch_size = 200000000000ULL;

    auto wrapper = duckdb::make_uniq<duckdb::ResultArrowArrayStreamWrapper>(
        std::move(result), huge_batch_size);

    ArrowArray batch;
    memset(&batch, 0, sizeof(batch));

    // This calls ArrowVarcharToStringViewData::Append which writes to NULL → SIGSEGV
    int rc = wrapper->stream.get_next(&wrapper->stream, &batch);
    if (rc != 0) {
        const char *err = wrapper->stream.get_last_error(&wrapper->stream);
        fprintf(stderr, "Arrow error: %s\n", err ? err : "unknown");
        return 1;
    }

    if (batch.release) {
        printf("Got batch with %lld rows\n", (long long)batch.length);
        batch.release(&batch);
    }

    printf("Success\n");
    return 0;
}
```

