On version 0.21 when attempting to import a CSV as specified in the documentation on https://duckdb.org/docs/data/csv I tried:

    sqlite> COPY request FROM '/Volumes/Space/dataFiles/requests/999-requests-00000-b16671eb.gz' ( FORMAT CSV_AUTO );
    Error: Catalog: Copy Function with name csv_auto does not exist!

So instead I used `INSERT INTO`:

    INSERT INTO request SELECT * FROM read_csv_auto('/Volumes/Space/dataFiles/requests/997-requests-00000-9a4ae116.gz');

Currently our string representation inside vectors is just a pointer to a null-terminated string. This means that for every single operation we perform on these strings we need to follow the pointer, and this also leads to many calls to `strlen` in our codebase. This is unfortunate because in many cases we already know the length of strings (e.g. when we read them from storage). We want to change the string representation to the following representation:

```cpp
struct string_t {
	char *data;
	uint32_t length;
};
```

The inclusion of a length field everywhere allows for more efficient operations (e.g. string comparisons can early abort when the length is different, and can use `memcmp` instead of `strcmp`). This has consequences for how strings are handled all over the execution engine, however, and thus is a reasonably sized change. I will start working on this soon.

