Hi, I'm facing a problem with version 0.2.4.
After some debug I found that sometimes blobs become empty after the checkpoint.
In my test I forced a checkpoint closing and reloading the DB in READ_WRITE mode.
Here is a simple program to see the problem:

````C++
#include <iostream>
#include "duckdb.hpp"
#include "duckdb/main/appender.hpp"

using namespace duckdb;

int main(int argc, char *argv[])
{
        DBConfig config;
	config.access_mode = AccessMode::READ_WRITE;

	{
		std::cout << "Creating TestBlob.duck...";
		DuckDB dbObj("TestBlob.duck", &config);
		Connection conObj(dbObj);
		std::cout << " Ok" << std::endl;

		conObj.Query("CREATE TABLE test(value BLOB);");

		unsigned char data[] = { 128, 230, 197, 71, '\0' };
		unsigned char data2[] = { 'C', 'I', 'A', '0', '\0' };

		conObj.BeginTransaction();
		{
			std::cout << "Appending data to test table" << std::endl;

			Appender appender(conObj, "test");

			appender.BeginRow();
			appender.Append(Value::BLOB((const_data_ptr_t)data, 4));
			appender.EndRow();

			appender.BeginRow();
			appender.Append(Value::BLOB((const_data_ptr_t)data2, 4));
			appender.EndRow();

			appender.Flush();
		}
		conObj.Commit();

		std::cout << "Reading test table" << std::endl;

		auto result = conObj.Query("SELECT * FROM test");

		result->Print();
	}

	{
		std::cout << "Execute checkpoint...";
		DuckDB dbObj("TestBlob.duck", &config);
		Connection conObj(dbObj);
		std::cout << " Ok" << std::endl;

		std::cout << "Reading test table" << std::endl;

		auto result = conObj.Query("SELECT * FROM test");

		result->Print();
	}
	return 0;
}
````

Output:

````
Creating TestBlob.duck... Ok
Appending data to test table
Reading test table
value
BLOB
[ Rows: 2]
\x80\xE6\xC5G
CIA0


Execute checkpoint... Ok
Reading test table
value
BLOB
[ Rows: 2]
NULL
CIA0
````

## What

`SegmentTree::TryGetSegmentIndex` in `src/include/duckdb/storage/table/segment_tree.hpp` (lines 207-242) does an unguarded `upper = index - 1` on `idx_t` (= `uint64_t`). When `index == 0` and `row_number < entry.row_start`, `upper` underflows to `UINT64_MAX`, the next loop iteration produces a huge `index`, and the bounds check on line 222 throws `InternalException("Segment tree index not found for row number ...")`.

PR #18585 ([link](https://github.com/duckdb/duckdb/pull/18585)) proposed exactly this defensive bounds fix and was closed in favor of fixing one specific upstream trigger via PR #18876. **However, the defensive code in `TryGetSegmentIndex` is still unchanged, and we just hit a new upstream trigger in production that #18876 does not cover.**

## Where (current main HEAD)

`src/include/duckdb/storage/table/segment_tree.hpp:207-242`:

```cpp
bool TryGetSegmentIndex(SegmentLock &l, idx_t row_number, idx_t &result) {
    ...
    idx_t lower = 0;
    idx_t upper = nodes.size() - 1;
    while (lower <= upper) {
        idx_t index = (lower + upper) / 2;
        if (index >= nodes.size()) {
            string segments;
            for (auto &entry : nodes) {
                segments += StringUtil::Format("Start %d Count %d",
                                               entry.row_start, entry.node->count.load());
            }
            throw InternalException("Segment tree index not found for row number %d\nSegments:%s",
                                    row_number, segments);
        }
        auto &entry = nodes[index];
        if (row_number < entry.row_start) {
            upper = index - 1;     // ← idx_t underflow when index == 0
        } else if (row_number >= entry.row_start + entry.node->count) {
            lower = index + 1;
        } else {
            result = index;
            return true;
        }
    }
    return false;
}
```

## New upstream trigger we observed (not covered by #18876)

We hit this twice in production (v1.4.4) within 6 hours on different pods. The exact error:

```
InternalException: Segment tree index not found for row number 367626
Segments:Start 368640 Count 0
```

Note the single segment with `Count=0` — i.e. the segment tree has exactly one node and that node is an **empty shell**.

### Reproduction conditions (high level)

1. DuckDB running in **in-memory mode**, **no `temp_directory` set** → no spill path
2. Several concurrent transactions doing `UPDATE ... FROM temp_table_N` against a high-row-count table
3. Memory pressure rises to the `memory_limit` (we set 18.6 GiB)
4. One transaction reaches `DuckTransaction::Commit()`. Inside:
   - `storage->Commit()` / `undo_buffer.Commit()` attempts to allocate a 256 KiB block → throws `OutOfMemoryException`
   - Catch block calls `undo_buffer.RevertCommit(...)`
5. RevertCommit walks down the chain:
   ```
   UndoBuffer::RevertCommit
     → DataTable::RevertAppend
       → RowGroupCollection::RevertAppendInternal
         → RowGroup::RevertAppend
           → StandardColumnData::RevertAppend
             → ColumnData::RevertAppend
               → SegmentTree::TryGetSegmentIndex(row_number=367626)
                 → idx_t underflow → throw InternalException
   ```
6. In `ColumnData::RevertAppend`, the early-return check `start_row >= last_segment->start + last_segment->count` is `367626 >= 368640 + 0 = 368640` → **false** (off by one — the segment is an empty shell at 368640, but `start_row` is below it). So we proceed into the binary search.
7. Binary search: `lower=0, upper=0, index=0`. `entry.row_start = 368640`. `row_number(367626) < 368640` → `upper = 0 - 1` → underflow → throw.

### Why this is a different trigger than #18876

PR #18876 patched `RevertAppend` to fully delete row groups instead of truncating to 0 rows, plus other fuzzer-found fixes. Our case is different:

- The empty-shell segment at `Start=368640, Count=0` was not produced by `RevertAppend` truncation. We believe it was left behind by the **OOM-aborted append in the first phase of commit** (a segment node was allocated, but the data write failed mid-flight and the node was left in the tree with count=0).
- `RevertAppend` is then called with `start_row=367626` — the row this transaction *intended* to start appending at, which is **before** the empty shell's `start=368640`.

This `start_row < smallest_segment.start` case is what triggers the binary search underflow. We have not seen this combination called out in #18585 / #18876 / #16331 / #4978.

## Combined impact with #22860

Because `DuckTransaction::Commit()` is declared `noexcept` and **does not** wrap the `RevertCommit` call in a try/catch (that's filed separately at #22860), this InternalException escapes the noexcept boundary → `__cxa_call_terminate` → `abort()` → entire process is killed.

So the practical effect is: any OOM during a commit on a memory-constrained in-memory database can kill the whole DuckDB process, taking down all attached databases. We hit this twice in 6 hours in prod.

## Asks

1. **(Primary)** Decide whether `TryGetSegmentIndex` warrants the defensive bounds fix proposed in PR #18585 given that recurring upstream triggers keep being discovered (this one, #16331, #4978, #3789, etc.). I am willing to send a new PR if defensive guarding is now acceptable.

2. **(Or)** Fix this specific upstream trigger in `ColumnData::RevertAppend` / `RowGroup::RevertAppend` so that an OOM-left empty-shell segment does not corner the binary search. One option: in `ColumnData::RevertAppend`, treat `start_row < last_segment->start` symmetrically to the existing "nothing was ever appended" early-return.

3. Either fix should also help the related closed reports: #16331, #4978, #3789, #7710, #622.

## Affected versions

- v1.4.4 (confirmed prod reproduction, two crashes within 6 hours)
- main HEAD as of 2026-05 (verified — bug still present)
- PR #18876 (merged Sep 2025) does NOT cover this trigger

Happy to share more details (stack pointers decoded via addr2line, full schema / formula context, etc.) if useful.

### What happens?

In the case of parallel execution, DuckDB sometimes undercounts a grouped probe-side join query when join_filter_pushdown installs a dynamic bloom filter. The same query is stable with PRAGMA threads=1, with join_filter_pushdown disabled, or when the grouped intermediate is materialized.

Reproduced on DuckDB 1.5.2 and on the bundled DuckDB version used through libduckdb-sys 1.10500.0 (v1.5.0).

The failure disappears with any of the following:

PRAGMA threads=1
SET disabled_optimizers='join_filter_pushdown'
forcing the relevant intermediate to materialize
disabling bloom-filter generation for this path


### To Reproduce

## Reproducing

Python script for reproducing:

```python
import duckdb

SQL = """
WITH c2 AS (
  SELECT p5.fromnode, p5.tonode, SUM(1.0) AS _freq
  FROM dblp p5
  JOIN dblp p6 ON p5.tonode = p6.fromnode
  GROUP BY p5.fromnode, p5.tonode
),
c3 AS (
  SELECT p4.fromnode, p4.tonode, SUM(c2._freq) AS _freq
  FROM c2
  JOIN dblp p4 ON p4.tonode = c2.fromnode
  GROUP BY p4.fromnode, p4.tonode
)
SELECT CAST(COALESCE(SUM(_freq), 0) AS BIGINT) FROM c3;
"""

SQL_MATERIALIZED = """
CREATE TEMP TABLE c2_mat AS
SELECT p5.fromnode, p5.tonode, SUM(1.0) AS _freq
FROM dblp p5
JOIN dblp p6 ON p5.tonode = p6.fromnode
GROUP BY p5.fromnode, p5.tonode;

SELECT CAST(COALESCE(SUM(_freq), 0) AS BIGINT)
FROM (
  SELECT p4.fromnode, p4.tonode, SUM(c2_mat._freq) AS _freq
  FROM c2_mat
  JOIN dblp p4 ON p4.tonode = c2_mat.fromnode
  GROUP BY p4.fromnode, p4.tonode
) t;
"""


def load_synthetic(con: duckdb.DuckDBPyConnection, edges: int = 200_000) -> None:
    con.execute(
        f"""
        CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE dblp AS
        SELECT
          CAST(LEAST(hash(i * 1000003) % {edges // 2 + edges // 4},
                     hash(i * 1000003 + 7) % {edges // 2 + edges // 4}) AS INT) AS fromnode,
          CAST(LEAST(hash(i * 9176) % {edges // 2 + edges},
                     hash(i * 9176 + 11) % {edges // 2 + edges}) AS INT) AS tonode
        FROM range({edges}) t(i)
        WHERE hash(i * 1000003) <> hash(i * 9176);
        """
    )


def run(configure, sql=SQL):
    con = duckdb.connect()
    load_synthetic(con)
    configure(con)
    result = con.execute(sql).fetchone()[0]
    con.close()
    return result


reference = run(lambda con: con.execute("PRAGMA threads=1"))
parallel = [run(lambda con: con.execute("PRAGMA threads=8")) for _ in range(20)]
parallel_no_pushdown = [
    run(lambda con: (con.execute("PRAGMA threads=8"), con.execute("SET disabled_optimizers='join_filter_pushdown'")))
    for _ in range(20)
]
materialized = [run(lambda con: con.execute("PRAGMA threads=8"), sql=SQL_MATERIALIZED) for _ in range(20)]

print("reference", reference)
print("parallel", sorted(set(parallel)))
print("parallel_no_pushdown", sorted(set(parallel_no_pushdown)))
print("materialized", sorted(set(materialized)))
```

Observed locally on DuckDB `1.5.2` (AMD Ryzen 9 3900X, 12 cores):

```text
reference 247699
parallel [218961, 241699, 245054, 247591, 247699]
parallel_no_pushdown [247699]
materialized [247699]
```

The expected behavior would be that the parallel result matches the single-threaded reference.
However, with `join_filter_pushdown` enabled under parallel execution, the query undercounts. 

## Possible cause

> [!WARNING]
> This investigation was done mostly with the help of AI tools, and I am providing it only for the possibility that it might help to identify the cause of the bug. I am not familiar with the DuckDB source code.


### Hypothesis

The likely failure mode is:
1. `join_filter_pushdown` publishes a `BFTableFilter` that references a live `BloomFilter`.
2. Bloom storage exists before build finalize is complete.
3. A descendant probe-side scan can start using that filter before finalize has fully populated it.
4. An incompletely built bloom filter yields false negatives.
5. Rows are dropped and the final count underflows.

This fits the observed behavior:
- disabling only `join_filter_pushdown` fixes the repro
- disabling bloom generation fixes the repro
- `threads=1` fixes the repro

### Possible fix

- add a `ready` flag to `BloomFilter`
- keep bloom filters fail-open until hash-join finalize completes
- mark the bloom filter ready in `HashJoinFinalizeEvent::FinishEvent`

This was validated locally (no undercounting).

Patch (v1.5.2, 8a58519):

```diff
diff --git a/src/execution/operator/join/physical_hash_join.cpp b/src/execution/operator/join/physical_hash_join.cpp
--- a/src/execution/operator/join/physical_hash_join.cpp	2026-04-14 15:07:18.625370619 +0000
+++ b/src/execution/operator/join/physical_hash_join.cpp	2026-04-14 15:07:54.907617338 +0000
@@ -581,6 +581,10 @@
 	void FinishEvent() override {
 		sink.hash_table->GetDataCollection().VerifyEverythingPinned();
 		sink.hash_table->finalized = true;
+		auto &bloom_filter = sink.hash_table->GetBloomFilter();
+		if (bloom_filter.IsInitialized()) {
+			bloom_filter.MarkReady();
+		}
 	}

 	static constexpr idx_t CHUNKS_PER_TASK = 64;
diff --git a/src/include/duckdb/planner/filter/bloom_filter.hpp b/src/include/duckdb/planner/filter/bloom_filter.hpp
--- a/src/include/duckdb/planner/filter/bloom_filter.hpp	2026-04-14 15:07:18.551370105 +0000
+++ b/src/include/duckdb/planner/filter/bloom_filter.hpp	2026-04-14 15:07:54.907617338 +0000
@@ -10,6 +10,7 @@

 #include "duckdb/common/serializer/deserializer.hpp"
 #include "duckdb/common/serializer/serializer.hpp"
+#include <atomic>
 #include "duckdb/planner/table_filter.hpp"
 #include "duckdb/common/types/value.hpp"
 #include "duckdb/planner/table_filter_state.hpp"
@@ -32,11 +33,20 @@
 		return initialized;
 	}

+	bool IsReady() const {
+		return ready.load(std::memory_order_acquire);
+	}
+
+	void MarkReady() {
+		ready.store(true, std::memory_order_release);
+	}
+
 private:
 	idx_t num_sectors;
 	uint64_t bitmask; // num_sectors - 1 -> used to get the sector offset

 	bool initialized = false;
+	std::atomic<bool> ready {false};
 	AllocatedData buf_;
 	uint64_t *bf;
 };
diff --git a/src/planner/filter/bloom_filter.cpp b/src/planner/filter/bloom_filter.cpp
--- a/src/planner/filter/bloom_filter.cpp	2026-04-14 15:07:18.542370043 +0000
+++ b/src/planner/filter/bloom_filter.cpp	2026-04-14 15:07:54.907617338 +0000
@@ -23,6 +23,7 @@
 	bf = reinterpret_cast<uint64_t *>((64ULL + reinterpret_cast<uint64_t>(buf_.get())) & ~63ULL);
 	std::fill_n(bf, num_sectors, 0);

+	ready.store(false, std::memory_order_relaxed);
 	initialized = true;
 }

@@ -71,10 +72,14 @@

 inline bool BloomFilter::LookupOne(const uint64_t hash) const {
 	D_ASSERT(initialized);
+	if (!IsReady()) {
+		return true;
+	}
 	const uint64_t bf_offset = hash & bitmask;
 	const uint64_t mask = GetMask(hash);
+	auto &slot = *reinterpret_cast<std::atomic<uint64_t> *>(&bf[bf_offset]);

-	return (bf[bf_offset] & mask) == mask;
+	return (slot.load(std::memory_order_acquire) & mask) == mask;
 }

