For older browser-based projects that use the `namespace` keyword (or `module` still), it can be inconvenient to interact with AMD/CommonJS/etc. style code.

It would be great if TypeScript could natively compile modern-style modules into the traditional namespaces. This would allow these older projects to interact near-natively with newer code without having to add an additional compilation step or including requirejs.

Here's a proposal for how it would look for a single-file output, where `src/Base.ts` and `src/Children/Child.ts` are written in the import style and compiled to a single file `dist/Compiled.js`:
### src/base.ts

_Original Import Style:_

``` typescript
export const Base = true;
```

_Equivalent Namespace Style:_

``` typescript
namespace MyProject {
    export const Base = true;
}
```
### src/Children/Child.ts

_Original Import Style:_

``` typescript
import { Base } from "../Base";

export const Child = Base.toString();
```

_Equivalent Namespace Style:_

``` typescript
/// <reference path="../Base.ts" />

namespace MyProject.Children {
    // Optionally:
    // import Base = MyProject.Base;
    export const Child = Base.toString();
}
```
### dist/Compiled.js

_Output JS in Namespace Style:_

``` javascript
var MyProject;
(function (MyProject) {
    MyProject.Base = true;
    var Children;
    (function (Children) {
        Children.Child = MyProject.Base.toString();
    })(Children || (Children = {}));
})(MyProject || (MyProject = {}));
```

_Equivalent Namespace Style:_

``` typescript
namespace MyProject {
    export const Base = true;

    namespace Children {
        export const Child = Base.toString();
    }
}
```
#### Errata
- This system assumes that file system directory path === namespace name & path.
- You could make the argument that this would encourage users to stay with this old namespace approach. I would make the counter-point that this would help spread TS adoption, and the benefits of moving to a require-style usage (more modular code, asynchronous loading, etc.) still hold true.
- The motivation for this is the pain of making browser-style distribution versions of a project that both work with requirejs and can be embedded in namespace-y sites.

