# Bug Report

When `strictPropertyInitialization=false`, if you have a class that uses a decorator somewhere, and the prototype is later modified to tweak the first field in the class, then the field becomes `undefined` instead of what the prototype says.

### 🔎 Search Terms

decorator, undefined

### 🕗 Version & Regression Information

- I was unable to test this on prior versions because non-experimental decorators are only available in 5.0+.

### ⏯ Playground Link

[Playground link with relevant code][(https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?#code/PTAEFkE9QYwewCYFNQHM5IM6gBZIE5JA)](https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?strictPropertyInitialization=false#code/KYDwDg9gTgLgBAMwK4DsDGMCWEVwCbBrQCGM0AFDMVAObAwBccxKAngDRxEoyiPNsAlHADeAXwBQEtABtiAZ3lwAYhAiiJcRGoBG1JiiQBbHcCgBuKVoACBIlFLRNcMEh0zMaOHqjlh4iUkJAHkdACtCGAA6BXlMGhRyVQgosCgIMhhWMGBOEW0IHyYAJgAGODFBS255CBlgKJkIGnIUYAB3FTU-KIRdaiqJIA)

### 💻 Code

<!-- Please post the relevant code sample here as well. This code and the Playground code should be the same, do not use separate examples -->
```ts
export function decorator(target: any, context: any) {}

class Foo {
  foobar: number;

  @decorator
  public bar() {}
}

Object.assign(Foo.prototype, { foobar: 20 });
console.log(new Foo().foobar);

```

### 🙁 Actual behavior

Prints `undefined`.
This is because the emitted JS sees this field as uninitialized, so sets it to `void 0`, which happens after the prototype was changed.
```ts
constructor() {
  this.foobar = (__runInitializers(this, _instanceExtraInitializers), void 0);
}
```

### 🙂 Expected behavior

Prints `20`

