Here is a faster version of _dictNextPower() in dict.c, using [Bit Manipulation Instruction Sets](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bit_Manipulation_Instruction_Sets).

```c
unsigned long _dictNextPower(unsigned long size)
{
	if (size <= DICT_HT_INITIAL_SIZE)
		return DICT_HT_INITIAL_SIZE;
	if (size >= LONG_MAX)
		return LONG_MAX;

  	/* size is already the power of 2 */
	if (size & (size - 1) == 0)
		return size;

    /* count leading zero bits */
	int nlz = __builtin_clzl(size);
	int w = sizeof(size) * 8;

	return 1lu << (w - nlz);
}
```

I use [google benchmark](https://github.com/google/benchmark) to measure the performance of the following code:

```c
for (unsigned long i = 0; i < 65536; ++i) {
	_dictNextPower(i);
}
```

|                  | CPU time  |
| ---------------- | --------- |
| original version | 472458 ns |
| new version      | 177176 ns |

Shall we use the faster version?

**The problem/use-case that the feature addresses**

While I use a module to do something with a fake slave, it's not so easy for the module to control how to expire the key.

**Description of the feature**

Actually, it will better if there is a way for module to prevent default key expire action, not just be notified.
A easy idea is to add a checking function in `propagateExpire`.
```C
    //in old propagateExpire
    robj *argv[2];

    argv[0] = lazy ? shared.unlink : shared.del;
    argv[1] = key;
    incrRefCount(argv[0]);
    incrRefCount(argv[1]);

    ...
    ...
    propagate(db->id,argv,2,PROPAGATE_AOF|PROPAGATE_REPL);
```
=>
```C
   //in new propagateExpire
    robj *argv;
    int argc;

    if (propateExpireModules(&argv, &argc)) {
        //the argv and argc is changed before
    } else {
        argv[0] = lazy ? shared.unlink : shared.del;
        argv[1] = key;
        incrRefCount(argv[0]);
        incrRefCount(argv[1]);
    }
    ...
    ...
    propagate(db->id,argv,argc,PROPAGATE_AOF|PROPAGATE_REPL);
```

